modules.how_it_works:
TFT COLOR DISPLAY (ST7735 160x80) — TWO DRAWING MODES:

CHOOSING A MODE:
- DIRECT MODE for games, animations, or anything with moving objects.
  Call setDirectMode(true) once in setup. Draws go straight to the screen.
- CANVAS MODE (default) for static dashboards, sensor readouts, or full-screen redraws.
  Pattern: clear() → draw everything → display(). Flicker-free.

DIRECT MODE RULES (CRITICAL — violating these causes flashing/slowness):
  1. Call setDirectMode(true) and fillScreen() ONCE in setup.
  2. NEVER call fillScreen(), clear(), or display() in the game loop. These redraw the ENTIRE screen and cause visible flashing.
  3. To move an object: erase its OLD position with a small fillRect (background color), then draw at the NEW position.
  4. For text that changes (score): erase the old text area with a small fillRect, then redraw the text.
  5. For static elements (ground line): draw once in setup or when entering a state, NOT every frame.
  6. When switching screens (title→playing, playing→gameover): call fillScreen() ONCE to clear, then draw the new screen.

DIRECT MODE EXAMPLE (game loop):
  // Save old positions BEFORE updating physics
  int oldDinoY = dinoY;
  int oldObsX = obsX;
  // Update physics...
  dinoY += velocity;
  obsX -= speed;
  // Erase old positions with small rects (NOT fillScreen!)
  fillRect(dinoX, oldDinoY, dinoW, dinoH, BG_COLOR);
  fillRect(oldObsX, obsTop, obsW, obsH, BG_COLOR);
  // Draw at new positions
  fillRect(dinoX, dinoY, dinoW, dinoH, DINO_COLOR);
  fillRect(obsX, obsTop, obsW, obsH, OBS_COLOR);

CANVAS MODE PATTERN (dashboards/UIs):
  // In loop: clear() → draw everything → display().

GENERAL RULES:
- print() takes ONE argument only. NEVER pass coordinates to print().
- To print at a position: setCursor(x, y) THEN print(value). Two separate calls.
- Or use displayText(text, x, y, size, color) for positioned text in one call.
- Available colors: ST7735_BLACK, ST7735_WHITE, ST7735_RED, ST7735_GREEN, ST7735_BLUE, ST7735_YELLOW, ST7735_CYAN, ST7735_MAGENTA, ST7735_ORANGE.
- Screen is 160x80 pixels.
- SMOOTH FONTS: For text larger than size 1, ALWAYS use smooth FreeType fonts instead of setTextSize().
  setTextSize(2+) scales the tiny 5x7 bitmap and looks blocky. Smooth fonts look much better.
  Usage: #include <Fonts/FreeSansBold12pt7b.h>, then setFont(&FreeSansBold12pt7b) before printing.
  After setFont(), always use setTextSize(1) — the font itself defines the size.
  IMPORTANT: With custom fonts, Y coordinate is the text BASELINE, not top-left. Add font height to Y.
  Available smooth fonts: FreeSans9pt7b, FreeSans12pt7b, FreeSansBold9pt7b, FreeSansBold12pt7b, FreeSansBold18pt7b, FreeSansBold24pt7b.
  To return to the small default font: setFont(NULL) then setTextSize(1).
  Example for big text: setFont(&FreeSansBold12pt7b); setTextSize(1); setCursor(10, 30); print("Hello");
  displayText() uses setTextSize() internally so AVOID it for large text. Use setFont + setCursor + print instead.

NEOPIXEL 3x3 LED GRID:
- setPixel(index, r, g, b) sets one LED. Index 0-8 in row-major order (0=top-left, 8=bottom-right).
- setAll(r, g, b) sets all 9 LEDs to the same color.
- MUST call show() after any color changes — without it nothing updates on the physical LEDs.
- setBrightness(value) controls global brightness. Call before show(). Hard-capped at 51 (20% of 255);
  requesting anything higher just gets clamped. Scale your brightness ramps to 0-51, not 0-255.
- clear() + show() turns all LEDs off.
- The driver drives BOTH the old RGB module (Line A) and the new RGBW module (Line B) in parallel,
  so user code is identical for either revision. Do not try to address the white channel directly
  — the RGB API already produces correct output on both revisions.
- setRow(row, r, g, b) and setColumn(col, r, g, b) for row=0-2, col=0-2.
- Keep animations at 20-50ms delay between frames for smooth visuals.
- PERFORMANCE: show() takes ~5ms on ESP32 (the driver writes to both module
  revisions every frame). Calling it in a tight loop, or on every tick of
  an input driver like a rotary encoder, will accumulate visible catch-up
  lag during fast user input.
  RULE 1: only call show() when the colors actually changed. Track a dirty
  flag or compare against the previous color, e.g.:
    if (r != lastR || g != lastG || b != lastB) {
      neopixel.setAll(r, g, b);
      neopixel.show();
      lastR = r; lastG = g; lastB = b;
    }
  RULE 2: for INTERACTIVE-INPUT loops (knob-to-color, slider-to-brightness,
  etc.) that can fire many times per second, coalesce LED updates to ~30Hz.
  Instead of calling show() on every loop tick, only actually push the
  current color when >=33ms have passed since the last show(). The input
  still polls fast; only the LED write is throttled. Example:
    unsigned long now = millis();
    if (dirty && now - lastShow >= 33) {
      neopixel.setAll(r, g, b);
      neopixel.show();
      lastShow = now;
      dirty = false;
    }
  For pulsing/breathing animations, cap the framerate the same way
  (every 30-50ms).
